CHAPTER VII
SERVICES FOR VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE
Article 13
(1) Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence, are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid services;
d. repatriation services;
e. psychological services; and
f. rehabilitation and social reintegration services.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with the Minimum Service Standards (SPM) established by the Government and Regional Government and implemented by SKPD and UPTD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual matters.
(3) In carrying out the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall cooperate with Government agencies, other Regional Governments, and the community.

CHAPTER VI
PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE
Article 10
(1) To prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government shall conduct empowerment and awareness-raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, counseling and/or guidance.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall undertake efforts as follows: a. increase the quantity and quality of education both formal, non-formal and informal;
b. increase accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement, and social services; c. open employment opportunities for women;
d. build community participation and concern towards the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence; e. build and provide a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. build networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. open complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence based at the sub-district level down to the village.

Article 3
(1) The objectives of implementing protection for women and children victims of violence are to:
a. prevent the occurrence of all forms of violence against women and children;
b. provide initial handling of victims;
c. provide health services for victims both physical and psychological; d. provide guarantees of protection, security, and legal certainty to women and children victims of violence, witnesses, and reporters; e. pursue recovery of victims and rehabilitation;
f. empower victims and reintegration;
g. conduct coordination and evaluation of handling violence against women and children.
(2) Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of: a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic neglect; and
e. restriction of public access.

Article 18
(1) Regional Government establishes partnerships with business entities in the protection of women and children from victims of violence.
(2) Partnership as regulated in paragraph (1) is conducted through:
a. notification of employment opportunity information for women victims of violence;
b. education and training for women victims of violence;
c. educational assistance for women and children victims of violence who have dropped out of their education;
d. fostering and increasing economic independence of women victims of violence;
e. business facilities and capital access.
(3) Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is set forth in a joint agreement

CHAPTER XI
GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION
Article 19
(1) Regional Government is obligated to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection of women and children from violence.
(2) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance;
c. education and training;
d. provision of facilities;
e. monitoring; and
f. evaluation.
(3) In order to realize the objectives of protection of women and children from acts of violence, guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards.

